# jenkinsci/support-core-plugin, CVE-2022-45383 (SECURITY-2804). The support
# bundle is controller-wide diagnostic and secret material, and the two
# entry points that hand it out authorize on a permission that is separable
# from controller administration:
#
#   SupportAction.getTarget()  -- the StaplerProxy check every /support/
#                                 request passes through
#       Jenkins.get().checkPermission(CREATE_BUNDLE);
#   SupportCommand.run()       -- the `support` CLI command
#       Jenkins.get().checkPermission(SupportPlugin.CREATE_BUNDLE);
#
# CREATE_BUNDLE is a plugin-declared hudson.security.Permission,
#
#   new Permission(SUPPORT_PERMISSIONS, "DownloadBundle", ...,
#       Jenkins.ADMINISTER, PermissionScope.JENKINS),
#
# so ADMINISTER implies it, but the grant runs the other way too: it is its
# own checkbox in the authorization matrix, grantable to any subject without
# Overall/Administer. The fixed revision (9b7a1d48db0f) swaps both checks -- and the two
# jelly `permission="${...CREATE_BUNDLE}"` attributes -- to Jenkins.ADMINISTER,
# and deprecates the permission. A subject holding only DownloadBundle could
# download and delete bundles at the parent; at the fixed revision it cannot. The row
# checks out end to end: the advisory's CVE is this SECURITY issue, the parent
# carries the vulnerable form, the remediation is present at the fixed revision.
#
# Re-measured against the current knowledge base, three `timeout 180
# ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 6GB` runs per revision: 148 findings /
# 131 at or above high on both 9b7a1d48db0f^ and 9b7a1d48db0f, identical inside
# each revision and identical between them once the five lines the fix prepends
# to SupportAction.java are accounted for. Same rule distribution (INJ-004 84,
# PATH-001 33, DOS-001 6, INJ-009 5, AUTH-079 4, RES-001 3, INJ-006 3,
# SEC-025 2, NUM-014 2, DESER-002 2, INJ-010 1, DESER-004 1, CRY-001 1,
# AUTH-101 1) and the same finding multiset modulo line numbers; the counts
# have both grown from the 122/105 recorded at first review, none of the new
# rules lands on either entry point. The full `-flags only` view tells the
# same story over 86 flags on both revisions: `core.AuthorizationCheck` is
# emitted at SupportAction.java:91 and SupportCommand.java:78 at the vulnerable
# revision and at :96 / :78 at the fixed one, each with the identical text
# `call=Jenkins.get.checkPermission via=java.controls/resolved`. The label
# carries no permission identity, so a vulnerable check and a correct one are
# the same fact.
#
# The counterweight test -- "does the fixed revision leave an API-documented call inside
# the method it guards" -- is satisfied but discriminates nothing: the call
# left inside getTarget() and run() at both revisions is checkPermission
# itself; the only token that changes is its argument constant, and keying on
# the presence of `Jenkins.ADMINISTER` keys on the fixed revision's own spelling. The
# same permission under its older `Hudson.ADMINISTER` name, a static-import
# alias, or any correct non-ADMINISTER check would re-flag. The jelly
# attributes discriminate no better: `permission="${app.ADMINISTER}"` is the
# fixed spelling, so a textpattern on the receiver name (`app` versus
# `action`/`it`) is the same fix-vocabulary key wearing a config route, and
# the corpus carries one more non-`app` spelling that is a correct granular
# permission (`permission="${it.REPLAY}"`).
#
# The collateral of the one candidate shape, "checks a permission other than
# Jenkins.ADMINISTER", enumerated by grep over the current corpus clones
# (140 Java files, 295 checkPermission sites): 52 Jenkins.ADMINISTER, then
# 13 Item.CONFIGURE (xldeploy-plugin, katalon-plugin), 1 Computer.CONFIGURE,
# 1 Item.CANCEL (workflow-cps-plugin, CpsFlowExecution.java:1531),
# 1 Permission.CREATE (xldeploy-plugin Credential.java:323), and roughly
# forty dynamic spellings (requiredPermission, p, permission, ...). A further
# share of the remainder is not Jenkins ACL at all -- undertow's
# SecurityManager.checkPermission(PERMISSION) and quarkus's
# SecurityIdentity.checkPermission(requiredPermission) share the method name
# with an unrelated API. Every one of the ACL sites is the documented intended
# use of hudson.security.Permission: a plugin declares a permission implied-by
# the strength its data warrants and checks that. Which strength a given
# endpoint's data warrants is the per-endpoint policy decision SECURITY-2804
# made for exactly these two sites; it is not encoded in the code, in the
# Permission JavaDoc, or in any taint path. Not an engine gap -- the call
# resolves and is labelled; the missing thing is a judgment no binding can
# carry without firing on the entire healthy Jenkins plugin corpus.
#
# The authorization-strength family, prior ranks 1514, 1535, 1540, 1556,
# 1557, 1593.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-precision-cost, both revisions rejected below;
# the pins are against VYQL-AUTH-079, the sensitive-operation audit rule that
# does fire elsewhere in this tree (SupportActionTest.java:261), and the first
# case proves that rule is live on this shape before the rejects assert it
# stays quiet once any checkPermission covers the endpoint.

test "rank1811: a destructive operation with no authorization check reports the audit rule"
  lang java
  file ProbeGrant.java
  expect VYQL-AUTH-079
  code
  ```
  import hudson.model.User;
  import jenkins.model.Jenkins;
  import org.kohsuke.stapler.StaplerRequest;
  import org.kohsuke.stapler.StaplerResponse;

  public class ProbeGrant {
      public void doPurge(StaplerRequest req, StaplerResponse rsp) throws Exception {
          User u = User.getById(req.getParameter("user"), true);
          u.delete();
          Jenkins.get().getAuthorizationStrategy().getACL().grant(null, "user");
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1811: the bundle root action authorizing every request on the plugin's own grantable permission"
  lang java
  file SupportAction.java
  reject VYQL-AUTH-079
  code
  ```
  import hudson.model.RootAction;
  import hudson.security.Permission;
  import jenkins.model.Jenkins;
  import org.kohsuke.stapler.StaplerProxy;
  import org.kohsuke.stapler.StaplerRequest;
  import org.kohsuke.stapler.StaplerResponse;

  import java.io.File;
  import java.io.IOException;
  import java.util.logging.Logger;

  public class SupportAction implements RootAction, StaplerProxy {
      public static final Permission CREATE_BUNDLE = SupportPlugin.CREATE_BUNDLE;
      private final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(SupportAction.class.getName());

      @Override
      public Object getTarget() {
          Jenkins.get().checkPermission(CREATE_BUNDLE);
          return this;
      }

      public String getIconFileName() {
          return "/plugin/support-core/images/support.svg";
      }

      public String getDisplayName() {
          return "Support";
      }

      public String getUrlName() {
          return "support";
      }

      public void doDownloadBundles(StaplerRequest req, StaplerResponse rsp) throws IOException {
          File fileToDownload = new File(SupportPlugin.getRootDirectory(), getSelectedBundle(req));
          rsp.setContentType("application/zip");
          rsp.addHeader("Content-Disposition", "inline; filename=" + fileToDownload.getName() + ";");
          FileUtils.copyFile(fileToDownload, rsp.getOutputStream());
          logger.info("Bundle " + fileToDownload.getAbsolutePath() + " successfully downloaded");
      }

      String getSelectedBundle(StaplerRequest req) {
          return req.getParameter("bundle");
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1811: the CLI support command authorizing on the plugin's own grantable permission"
  lang java
  file SupportCommand.java
  reject VYQL-AUTH-079
  code
  ```
  import hudson.cli.CLIAction;
  import hudson.cli.CLICommand;
  import jenkins.model.Jenkins;

  import java.io.OutputStream;
  import java.util.ArrayList;
  import java.util.List;

  public class SupportCommand extends CLICommand implements CLIAction {
      @Override
      public String getName() {
          return "support";
      }

      @Override
      protected int run() throws Exception {
          Jenkins.get().checkPermission(SupportPlugin.CREATE_BUNDLE);
          List<Object> selected = new ArrayList<>();
          for (Object c : SupportPlugin.getComponents()) {
              selected.add(c);
          }
          OutputStream os = stdout;
          SupportPlugin.setRequesterAuthentication(Jenkins.getAuthentication());
          try {
              SupportPlugin.writeBundle(os, selected);
          } finally {
              SupportPlugin.clearRequesterAuthentication();
          }
          return 0;
      }
  }
  ```

